{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791981971471,"sessionID":"ses_ec58e7febffeRT8OnzlxprItd6","part":{"partID":"prt_13a71ae07001FhRKdJgVLTi2Gq","sessionID":"ses_ec58e7febffeRT8OnzlxprItd6","messageID":"msg_13a71a976001x045ew6p6lUXWp","type":"tool","id":"toolu_vrtx_01FSQP9VhqqWarsKR5KxPcVn","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2-regression/cases/case-015-implementation-temptation/artifacts/result.md","content":"# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** A search system that returns matching records quickly,\npresenting the newest matching records to the user after a search is submitted.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The search system — the component that accepts a\nuser search submission and returns an ordered list of matching records.\n\n**Design ideas embedded in the source (separated from obligations):**\nThe source proposes two specific implementation mechanisms:\n- A Redis cache with a five-minute TTL\n- GraphQL as the query interface\n\nThese are design choices, not externally observable behaviors. A verifier\ncannot observe \"Redis\" or \"GraphQL\" at the user-facing boundary; they can\nobserve response latency, result ordering, and result freshness. These\nimplementation details are kept out of the normative sentences below.\n\n**Behavioral obligations extracted from the source:**\n1. When a user submits a search, the search system shall return matching\n   records.\n2. The results shall be ordered so that the newest matching records appear\n   first.\n3. Results shall return quickly (unquantified — flagged as needing\n   clarification).\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 (Candidate 1 — response time).** The source says results should return\n\"quickly.\" What is the maximum acceptable elapsed time, measured from when the\nuser submits a search to when the first results are visible? What threshold\ndistinguishes pass from fail, and does it differ for cached versus uncached\nresponses?\n\n**Q2 (Candidate 2 — sort order definition).** \"Newest\" matching records: which\ntimestamp defines recency — record creation time, last-modification time, or\nanother field? If two records share the same timestamp, what is the tie-break\nrule?\n\n**Q3 (Candidate 2 — result set scope).** Should all matching records be\nreturned and sorted, or should the system return only a subset (for example, a\nfirst page)? If a subset, what determines which records are included before\nsorting?\n\n**Q4 (Candidate 1 — what constitutes a \"matching\" record).** What makes a\nrecord match a search? Is matching defined by full-text, field-level\ncomparison, prefix, relevance score, or another rule? The answer determines\nwhether the response is verifiable.\n\n**Q5 (Candidate 1 — empty results).** When a submitted search matches no\nrecords, what shall the system return? An empty result set, an informational\nmessage, or some other response?\n\n**Q6 (general — stale results acceptability).** The source implies results may\nbe served from a cache. Is it acceptable to return results that do not reflect\nrecords created or modified after the cache was populated? If so, what is the\nmaximum acceptable staleness window?\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a user submits a search, the search system shall return\n  all records that match the submitted search criteria within [unresolved:\n  maximum response time].\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source requests that search results\n  \"return quickly.\" The user-visible obligation is that the system responds to\n  a submitted search with matching records. The mechanism proposed in the source\n  (Redis cache, GraphQL) is a design idea, not an observable behavior; it is\n  kept out of the normative sentence. \"Quickly\" is unquantified and blocks the\n  verification contract.\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The search system, at the interface visible to the\n    submitting user.\n  - Responsible system: Search system.\n  - Actor: User (submitting a search).\n  - Affected interfaces: The user-facing search interface (submission and\n    response).\n  - Inputs and data definitions: A submitted search — the specific form\n    (keyword string, structured query, filters) is unresolved (see Q4). The\n    record set to search is assumed to be the system's persistent record store;\n    scope is unresolved.\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is user\n    submission of a search. No precondition other than the system being\n    operational is stated; authentication or authorization requirements are\n    unresolved.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — a set of\n    matching records is returned. Alternate — no matching records exist (see\n    Q5, unresolved). Failure — system or dependency error response behavior is\n    unresolved.\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: Maximum\n    acceptable elapsed time from submission to first results visible is\n    unresolved (Q1). Whether the response is a complete set or a page is\n    unresolved (Q3).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Matching\" is\n    undefined (Q4). \"Quickly\" is unquantified (Q1). Acceptable result staleness\n    is unresolved (Q6).\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A populated record store; a user\n    submits a search query.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: A response containing a set of records is\n    returned at the user-facing boundary; elapsed time is measurable from\n    submission to response receipt.\n  - Expected result: The returned records all satisfy the match criterion;\n    elapsed time is within the threshold.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing:\n    Unresolved — the response-time threshold (Q1) and the definition of\n    \"match\" (Q4) must be established before pass/fail can be determined.\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Zero-result searches (Q5),\n    maximum record-set size, and dependency-failure behavior are unresolved.\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A timer\n    measuring elapsed time from submission event to response receipt at the\n    user boundary; a defined match oracle.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a user submits a search, the search system shall\n  present the matching records ordered by [unresolved: creation time |\n  last-modification time | other timestamp] from newest to oldest.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source states results should show \"the\n  newest matching records.\" This is a sort-order obligation on the result\n  presentation. \"Newest\" is ambiguous — the timestamp field and tie-break rule\n  are unresolved (Q2). \"Show the newest\" could also mean return only the top-N\n  newest rather than all matches sorted; that scope question is unresolved (Q3).\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The search system, at the user-facing response boundary.\n  - Responsible system: Search system.\n  - Actor: User (receiving search results).\n  - Affected interfaces: The user-facing result presentation interface.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The set of records matching the submitted\n    search (as defined by Candidate 1). Each record must expose a timestamp\n    field whose definition is unresolved (Q2).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the same\n    user search submission as Candidate 1. The sort obligation applies to the\n    result set produced in response to that submission.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — records are\n    presented with the newest first. Alternate — a single matching record\n    requires no ordering; an empty result set requires no ordering. Tie-break\n    behavior for equal timestamps is unresolved (Q2).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: The timestamp\n    field and its unit (epoch ms, ISO 8601, etc.) are unresolved. Whether all\n    matches or only top-N are presented is unresolved (Q3).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Newest\" (Q2), scope\n    of returned results (Q3), tie-break rule (Q2).\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A record store containing multiple\n    matching records with distinct and equal timestamps; a user submits a search\n    that matches those records.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The ordered sequence of records in the\n    response, inspectable at the user-facing boundary.\n  - Expected result: Records appear in descending timestamp order (newest\n    first); tie-break ordering is consistent with the agreed rule.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing:\n    Unresolved — the timestamp field definition (Q2) and tie-break rule must be\n    established. Pass requires that the first record in the response has a\n    timestamp ≥ all others in the same response.\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Single matching record; all records\n    sharing the same timestamp; empty result set.\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Access to\n    the timestamp field value of each returned record at the response boundary,\n    or an agreed oracle that can evaluate ordering correctness.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes, provided the timestamp field\n    is included in the response.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**S1 — Failure-path companion (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* What the search system shall do when a dependency required\nto produce results is unavailable (e.g., the record store is unreachable).\n*Reason:* Without a defined failure response, two implementers will choose\ndifferent visible behaviors (error message, partial results, silent empty set),\nmaking the system unverifiable at the boundary.\n*Suggested form (provisional):* If the search system cannot retrieve records\nwithin [unresolved: timeout], then the search system shall return an error\nresponse that does not present partial results as complete.\n\n**S2 — Boundary companion (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* Behavior when the submitted search is empty, malformed, or\nexceeds a defined length limit.\n*Reason:* The source does not constrain the input. An implementer must decide\nunilaterally whether to reject, interpret, or pass through these inputs.\n*Suggested form (provisional):* If the submitted search criteria are empty or\nmalformed, then the search system shall return an informative response that\nidentifies the input problem without exposing internal system detail.\n\n**S3 — Operational companion (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* A quantified response-time obligation.\n*Reason:* \"Quickly\" is the stated goal. Without a measurable threshold the\nrequirement is unverifiable. This companion captures the performance obligation\nonce Q1 is answered.\n*Suggested form (provisional, pending Q1):* When a user submits a search, the\nsearch system shall return the first results within [agreed threshold, e.g.,\n2 seconds] as measured from submission to first result visible at the\nuser-facing boundary.\n\n**S4 — Optional consideration (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* Whether results served from a cache may be stale relative\nto the current record store state, and what the maximum acceptable staleness\nwindow is.\n*Reason:* The source implies caching. If stale results are permissible, this\nis a visible behavioral contract (users may not see records created after the\ncache was populated). If not permissible, the cache TTL is an internal tuning\ndetail with no behavioral relevance.\n*Ask:* Is serving results that do not reflect records created or modified\nwithin the last [N minutes] acceptable? (See Q6.)\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n**Finding 1 — Gap: quantified performance obligation is absent.**\nCandidate 1 references a response-time goal inherited from \"quickly\" in the\nsource, but no measurable threshold appears in either candidate. S3 is\nsuggested to close this gap once Q1 is answered.\n\n**Finding 2 — Overlap risk: Candidates 1 and 2 share the same trigger.**\nBoth candidates are triggered by \"user submits a search.\" They describe\ndifferent responses (record retrieval vs. result ordering) and are intentionally\nsplit because they can be implemented and verified independently. No conflict;\nthe split is deliberate. The combined behavior is: retrieve matching records\nAND present them ordered newest-first.\n\n**Finding 3 — Precedence question: cache staleness vs. result completeness.**\nIf a caching layer is used (the source's stated design intent), results\nreturned by Candidate 1 may not include records added after the cache was\npopulated. This creates a potential tension between the completeness of\n\"all matching records\" (Candidate 1) and any staleness window accepted in S4.\nResolution requires an answer to Q6 and an explicit staleness bound or a\nrequirement that results reflect the state of the record store at the time of\nsubmission.\n\n**Finding 4 — Gap: empty-result behavior is undefined.**\nNeither candidate specifies what the system returns when no records match the\nsubmitted search. S2 partially addresses malformed input; the empty-result\ncase is covered by Q5 and needs an explicit requirement.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1.** \"Redis cache\" and \"GraphQL\" in the source are design proposals, not\nexternally observable behavioral obligations. They are treated as rationale for\nthe performance goal and are excluded from normative sentences. If either is\nintended as an external interface contract (e.g., the client is required to\nspeak GraphQL), that changes the system boundary and an interface companion\nrequirement would be needed.\n\n**A2.** \"Newest matching records\" is interpreted as a sort order over the full\nmatching result set (newest first), not as a filter that returns only the\ntop-N most recent records. This interpretation may be wrong; Q3 seeks\nconfirmation.\n\n**A3.** \"After a user submits a search\" is interpreted as a discrete\nevent-driven trigger, not an ongoing state. If the system is expected to\ncontinuously update results as new records arrive (live/streaming search), the\npattern would change to state-driven or complex, and additional behavior would\nneed to be defined.\n\n**A4.** The system boundary is the user-facing search interface. If the search\nsystem also exposes a machine-to-machine API boundary, additional interface\ncompanion requirements may be needed.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe source mixes two kinds of content:\n\n1. **Observable behavioral goals** — return matching records quickly, show the\n   newest first. These are the basis for Candidates 1 and 2.\n\n2. **Implementation design ideas** — use Redis with a five-minute TTL, use\n   GraphQL. These explain *why* the requester expects good performance but do\n   not themselves describe user-visible behavior. A verifier cannot observe\n   \"Redis\" or \"GraphQL\" at the user boundary. Including them in normative\n   sentences would constrain the implementation without constraining the\n   behavior, which is the opposite of good requirements practice.\n\nThe five-minute TTL, if it becomes a behavioral contract (e.g., users must not\nsee records more than five minutes out of date), should be captured as a\nstaleness bound in a supporting requirement (see S4 and Q6), not as an\nimplementation detail.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking item |\n|-----------|--------|---------------|\n| Candidate 1 | needs clarification | Q1 (response-time threshold), Q4 (match definition), Q5 (empty-result response), Q6 (staleness) |\n| Candidate 2 | needs clarification | Q2 (timestamp field and tie-break), Q3 (full set vs. top-N) |\n\nNeither candidate is `ready for review`. Both verification contracts contain\nunresolved items that prevent a verifier from determining pass or fail. The\nprimary blocking questions are Q1 (performance threshold) and Q2 (sort key\ndefinition). Once those are answered and the contracts are completed, the\ncandidates should be re-evaluated against both the implementability and\nverification gates before advancing.\n"},"output":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2-regression/cases/case-015-implementation-temptation/artifacts/result.md\", \"content\": \"# Requirements Elicitation Package\\n\\n## Understanding\\n\\n**Requested outcome:** A search system that returns matching records quickly,\\npresenting the newest matching records to the user after a search is submitted.\\n\\n**Proposed system boundary:** The search system — the component that accepts a\\nuser search submission and returns an ordered list of matching records.\\n\\n**Design ideas embedded in the source (separated from obligations):**\\nThe source proposes two specific implementation mechanisms:\\n- A Redis cache with a five-minute TTL\\n- GraphQL as the query interface\\n\\nThese are design choices, not externally observable behaviors. A verifier\\ncannot observe \\\"Redis\\\" or \\\"GraphQL\\\" at the user-facing boundary; they can\\nobserve response latency, result ordering, and result freshness. These\\nimplementation details are kept out of the normative sentences below.\\n\\n**Behavioral obligations extracted from the source:**\\n1. When a user submits a search, the search system shall return matching\\n   records.\\n2. The results shall be ordered so that the newest matching records appear\\n   first.\\n3. Results shall return quickly (unquantified — flagged as needing\\n   clarification).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clarifying Questions\\n\\n**Q1 (Candidate 1 — response time).** The source says results should return\\n\\\"quickly.\\\" What is the maximum acceptable elapsed time, measured from when the\\nuser submits a search to when the first results are visible? What threshold\\ndistinguishes pass from fail, and does it differ for cached versus uncached\\nresponses?\\n\\n**Q2 (Candidate 2 — sort order definition).** \\\"Newest\\\" matching records: which\\ntimestamp defines recency — record creation time, last-modification time, or\\nanother field? If two records share the same timestamp, what is the tie-break\\nrule?\\n\\n**Q3 (Candidate 2 — result set scope).** Should all matching records be\\nreturned and sorted, or should the system return only a subset (for example, a\\nfirst page)? If a subset, what determines which records are included before\\nsorting?\\n\\n**Q4 (Candidate 1 — what constitutes a \\\"matching\\\" record).** What makes a\\nrecord match a search? Is matching defined by full-text, field-level\\ncomparison, prefix, relevance score, or another rule? The answer determines\\nwhether the response is verifiable.\\n\\n**Q5 (Candidate 1 — empty results).** When a submitted search matches no\\nrecords, what shall the system return? An empty result set, an informational\\nmessage, or some other response?\\n\\n**Q6 (general — stale results acceptability).** The source implies results may\\nbe served from a cache. Is it acceptable to return results that do not reflect\\nrecords created or modified after the cache was populated? If so, what is the\\nmaximum acceptable staleness window?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Candidate Requirements\\n\\n### Candidate 1\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Event-driven\\n- **Requirement:** When a user submits a search, the search system shall return\\n  all records that match the submitted search criteria within [unresolved:\\n  maximum response time].\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source requests that search results\\n  \\\"return quickly.\\\" The user-visible obligation is that the system responds to\\n  a submitted search with matching records. The mechanism proposed in the source\\n  (Redis cache, GraphQL) is a design idea, not an observable behavior; it is\\n  kept out of the normative sentence. \\\"Quickly\\\" is unquantified and blocks the\\n  verification contract.\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: The search system, at the interface visible to the\\n    submitting user.\\n  - Responsible system: Search system.\\n  - Actor: User (submitting a search).\\n  - Affected interfaces: The user-facing search interface (submission and\\n    response).\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: A submitted search — the specific form\\n    (keyword string, structured query, filters) is unresolved (see Q4). The\\n    record set to search is assumed to be the system's persistent record store;\\n    scope is unresolved.\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is user\\n    submission of a search. No precondition other than the system being\\n    operational is stated; authentication or authorization requirements are\\n    unresolved.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — a set of\\n    matching records is returned. Alternate — no matching records exist (see\\n    Q5, unresolved). Failure — system or dependency error response behavior is\\n    unresolved.\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: Maximum\\n    acceptable elapsed time from submission to first results visible is\\n    unresolved (Q1). Whether the response is a complete set or a page is\\n    unresolved (Q3).\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"Matching\\\" is\\n    undefined (Q4). \\\"Quickly\\\" is unquantified (Q1). Acceptable result staleness\\n    is unresolved (Q6).\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A populated record store; a user\\n    submits a search query.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: A response containing a set of records is\\n    returned at the user-facing boundary; elapsed time is measurable from\\n    submission to response receipt.\\n  - Expected result: The returned records all satisfy the match criterion;\\n    elapsed time is within the threshold.\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing:\\n    Unresolved — the response-time threshold (Q1) and the definition of\\n    \\\"match\\\" (Q4) must be established before pass/fail can be determined.\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Zero-result searches (Q5),\\n    maximum record-set size, and dependency-failure behavior are unresolved.\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A timer\\n    measuring elapsed time from submission event to response receipt at the\\n    user boundary; a defined match oracle.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate 2\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Event-driven\\n- **Requirement:** When a user submits a search, the search system shall\\n  present the matching records ordered by [unresolved: creation time |\\n  last-modification time | other timestamp] from newest to oldest.\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source states results should show \\\"the\\n  newest matching records.\\\" This is a sort-order obligation on the result\\n  presentation. \\\"Newest\\\" is ambiguous — the timestamp field and tie-break rule\\n  are unresolved (Q2). \\\"Show the newest\\\" could also mean return only the top-N\\n  newest rather than all matches sorted; that scope question is unresolved (Q3).\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: The search system, at the user-facing response boundary.\\n  - Responsible system: Search system.\\n  - Actor: User (receiving search results).\\n  - Affected interfaces: The user-facing result presentation interface.\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The set of records matching the submitted\\n    search (as defined by Candidate 1). Each record must expose a timestamp\\n    field whose definition is unresolved (Q2).\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the same\\n    user search submission as Candidate 1. The sort obligation applies to the\\n    result set produced in response to that submission.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — records are\\n    presented with the newest first. Alternate — a single matching record\\n    requires no ordering; an empty result set requires no ordering. Tie-break\\n    behavior for equal timestamps is unresolved (Q2).\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: The timestamp\\n    field and its unit (epoch ms, ISO 8601, etc.) are unresolved. Whether all\\n    matches or only top-N are presented is unresolved (Q3).\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"Newest\\\" (Q2), scope\\n    of returned results (Q3), tie-break rule (Q2).\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A record store containing multiple\\n    matching records with distinct and equal timestamps; a user submits a search\\n    that matches those records.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The ordered sequence of records in the\\n    response, inspectable at the user-facing boundary.\\n  - Expected result: Records appear in descending timestamp order (newest\\n    first); tie-break ordering is consistent with the agreed rule.\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing:\\n    Unresolved — the timestamp field definition (Q2) and tie-break rule must be\\n    established. Pass requires that the first record in the response has a\\n    timestamp ≥ all others in the same response.\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Single matching record; all records\\n    sharing the same timestamp; empty result set.\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Access to\\n    the timestamp field value of each returned record at the response boundary,\\n    or an agreed oracle that can evaluate ordering correctness.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes, provided the timestamp field\\n    is included in the response.\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\\n\\n**S1 — Failure-path companion (Candidate 1).**\\n*Missing behavior:* What the search system shall do when a dependency required\\nto produce results is unavailable (e.g., the record store is unreachable).\\n*Reason:* Without a defined failure response, two implementers will choose\\ndifferent visible behaviors (error message, partial results, silent empty set),\\nmaking the system unverifiable at the boundary.\\n*Suggested form (provisional):* If the search system cannot retrieve records\\nwithin [unresolved: timeout], then the search system shall return an error\\nresponse that does not present partial results as complete.\\n\\n**S2 — Boundary companion (Candidate 1).**\\n*Missing behavior:* Behavior when the submitted search is empty, malformed, or\\nexceeds a defined length limit.\\n*Reason:* The source does not constrain the input. An implementer must decide\\nunilaterally whether to reject, interpret, or pass through these inputs.\\n*Suggested form (provisional):* If the submitted search criteria are empty or\\nmalformed, then the search system shall return an informative response that\\nidentifies the input problem without exposing internal system detail.\\n\\n**S3 — Operational companion (Candidate 1).**\\n*Missing behavior:* A quantified response-time obligation.\\n*Reason:* \\\"Quickly\\\" is the stated goal. Without a measurable threshold the\\nrequirement is unverifiable. This companion captures the performance obligation\\nonce Q1 is answered.\\n*Suggested form (provisional, pending Q1):* When a user submits a search, the\\nsearch system shall return the first results within [agreed threshold, e.g.,\\n2 seconds] as measured from submission to first result visible at the\\nuser-facing boundary.\\n\\n**S4 — Optional consideration (Candidate 1).**\\n*Missing behavior:* Whether results served from a cache may be stale relative\\nto the current record store state, and what the maximum acceptable staleness\\nwindow is.\\n*Reason:* The source implies caching. If stale results are permissible, this\\nis a visible behavioral contract (users may not see records created after the\\ncache was populated). If not permissible, the cache TTL is an internal tuning\\ndetail with no behavioral relevance.\\n*Ask:* Is serving results that do not reflect records created or modified\\nwithin the last [N minutes] acceptable? (See Q6.)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Consistency Findings\\n\\n**Finding 1 — Gap: quantified performance obligation is absent.**\\nCandidate 1 references a response-time goal inherited from \\\"quickly\\\" in the\\nsource, but no measurable threshold appears in either candidate. S3 is\\nsuggested to close this gap once Q1 is answered.\\n\\n**Finding 2 — Overlap risk: Candidates 1 and 2 share the same trigger.**\\nBoth candidates are triggered by \\\"user submits a search.\\\" They describe\\ndifferent responses (record retrieval vs. result ordering) and are intentionally\\nsplit because they can be implemented and verified independently. No conflict;\\nthe split is deliberate. The combined behavior is: retrieve matching records\\nAND present them ordered newest-first.\\n\\n**Finding 3 — Precedence question: cache staleness vs. result completeness.**\\nIf a caching layer is used (the source's stated design intent), results\\nreturned by Candidate 1 may not include records added after the cache was\\npopulated. This creates a potential tension between the completeness of\\n\\\"all matching records\\\" (Candidate 1) and any staleness window accepted in S4.\\nResolution requires an answer to Q6 and an explicit staleness bound or a\\nrequirement that results reflect the state of the record store at the time of\\nsubmission.\\n\\n**Finding 4 — Gap: empty-result behavior is undefined.**\\nNeither candidate specifies what the system returns when no records match the\\nsubmitted search. S2 partially addresses malformed input; the empty-result\\ncase is covered by Q5 and needs an explicit requirement.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n**A1.** \\\"Redis cache\\\" and \\\"GraphQL\\\" in the source are design proposals, not\\nexternally observable behavioral obligations. They are treated as rationale for\\nthe performance goal and are excluded from normative sentences. If either is\\nintended as an external interface contract (e.g., the client is required to\\nspeak GraphQL), that changes the system boundary and an interface companion\\nrequirement would be needed.\\n\\n**A2.** \\\"Newest matching records\\\" is interpreted as a sort order over the full\\nmatching result set (newest first), not as a filter that returns only the\\ntop-N most recent records. This interpretation may be wrong; Q3 seeks\\nconfirmation.\\n\\n**A3.** \\\"After a user submits a search\\\" is interpreted as a discrete\\nevent-driven trigger, not an ongoing state. If the system is expected to\\ncontinuously update results as new records arrive (live/streaming search), the\\npattern would change to state-driven or complex, and additional behavior would\\nneed to be defined.\\n\\n**A4.** The system boundary is the user-facing search interface. If the search\\nsystem also exposes a machine-to-machine API boundary, additional interface\\ncompanion requirements may be needed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rationale and Context\\n\\nThe source mixes two kinds of content:\\n\\n1. **Observable behavioral goals** — return matching records quickly, show the\\n   newest first. These are the basis for Candidates 1 and 2.\\n\\n2. **Implementation design ideas** — use Redis with a five-minute TTL, use\\n   GraphQL. These explain *why* the requester expects good performance but do\\n   not themselves describe user-visible behavior. A verifier cannot observe\\n   \\\"Redis\\\" or \\\"GraphQL\\\" at the user boundary. Including them in normative\\n   sentences would constrain the implementation without constraining the\\n   behavior, which is the opposite of good requirements practice.\\n\\nThe five-minute TTL, if it becomes a behavioral contract (e.g., users must not\\nsee records more than five minutes out of date), should be captured as a\\nstaleness bound in a supporting requirement (see S4 and Q6), not as an\\nimplementation detail.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Review Status\\n\\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking item |\\n|-----------|--------|---------------|\\n| Candidate 1 | needs clarification | Q1 (response-time threshold), Q4 (match definition), Q5 (empty-result response), Q6 (staleness) |\\n| Candidate 2 | needs clarification | Q2 (timestamp field and tie-break), Q3 (full set vs. top-N) |\\n\\nNeither candidate is `ready for review`. Both verification contracts contain\\nunresolved items that prevent a verifier from determining pass or fail. The\\nprimary blocking questions are Q1 (performance threshold) and Q2 (sort key\\ndefinition). Once those are answered and the contracts are completed, the\\ncandidates should be re-evaluated against both the implementability and\\nverification gates before advancing.\\n\"}"},"time":{"start":1791981891079,"end":1791981971471}}}}
