{"type":"text","timestamp":1791982039703,"sessionID":"ses_ec58e7febffeRT8OnzlxprItd6","part":{"id":"prt_13a72ecd9001oTm3vVLWY8gSYy","sessionID":"ses_ec58e7febffeRT8OnzlxprItd6","messageID":"msg_13a72e898001RC8CAuc28XxGtE","type":"text","text":"---\n\n# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** A search system that returns matching records quickly, presenting the newest matching records to the user after a search is submitted.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The search system — the component that accepts a user search submission and returns an ordered list of matching records.\n\n**Design ideas embedded in the source (separated from obligations):**\nThe source proposes two specific implementation mechanisms:\n- A Redis cache with a five-minute TTL\n- GraphQL as the query interface\n\nThese are design choices, not externally observable behaviors. A verifier cannot observe \"Redis\" or \"GraphQL\" at the user-facing boundary; they can observe response latency, result ordering, and result freshness. These implementation details are kept out of the normative sentences below.\n\n**Behavioral obligations extracted from the source:**\n1. When a user submits a search, the search system shall return matching records.\n2. The results shall be ordered so that the newest matching records appear first.\n3. Results shall return quickly (unquantified — flagged as needing clarification).\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 (Candidate 1 — response time).** The source says results should return \"quickly.\" What is the maximum acceptable elapsed time, measured from when the user submits a search to when the first results are visible? What threshold distinguishes pass from fail, and does it differ for cached versus uncached responses?\n\n**Q2 (Candidate 2 — sort order definition).** \"Newest\" matching records: which timestamp defines recency — record creation time, last-modification time, or another field? If two records share the same timestamp, what is the tie-break rule?\n\n**Q3 (Candidate 2 — result set scope).** Should all matching records be returned and sorted, or should the system return only a subset (for example, a first page)? If a subset, what determines which records are included before sorting?\n\n**Q4 (Candidate 1 — what constitutes a \"matching\" record).** What makes a record match a search? Is matching defined by full-text, field-level comparison, prefix, relevance score, or another rule? The answer determines whether the response is verifiable.\n\n**Q5 (Candidate 1 — empty results).** When a submitted search matches no records, what shall the system return? An empty result set, an informational message, or some other response?\n\n**Q6 (general — stale results acceptability).** The source implies results may be served from a cache. Is it acceptable to return results that do not reflect records created or modified after the cache was populated? If so, what is the maximum acceptable staleness window?\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a user submits a search, the search system shall return all records that match the submitted search criteria within [unresolved: maximum response time].\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source requests that search results \"return quickly.\" The user-visible obligation is that the system responds to a submitted search with matching records. The mechanism proposed in the source (Redis cache, GraphQL) is a design idea, not an observable behavior; it is kept out of the normative sentence. \"Quickly\" is unquantified and blocks the verification contract.\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The search system, at the interface visible to the submitting user.\n  - Responsible system: Search system.\n  - Actor: User (submitting a search).\n  - Affected interfaces: The user-facing search interface (submission and response).\n  - Inputs and data definitions: A submitted search — the specific form (keyword string, structured query, filters) is unresolved (Q4). The record set to search is assumed to be the system's persistent record store; scope is unresolved.\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is user submission of a search. No precondition other than the system being operational is stated; authentication or authorization requirements are unresolved.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — a set of matching records is returned. Alternate — no matching records exist (Q5, unresolved). Failure — system or dependency error response behavior is unresolved.\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: Maximum acceptable elapsed time from submission to first results visible is unresolved (Q1). Whether the response is a complete set or a page is unresolved (Q3).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Matching\" is undefined (Q4). \"Quickly\" is unquantified (Q1). Acceptable result staleness is unresolved (Q6).\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A populated record store; a user submits a search query.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: A response containing a set of records is returned at the user-facing boundary; elapsed time is measurable from submission to response receipt.\n  - Expected result: The returned records all satisfy the match criterion; elapsed time is within the threshold.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Unresolved — the response-time threshold (Q1) and the definition of \"match\" (Q4) must be established before pass/fail can be determined.\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Zero-result searches (Q5), maximum record-set size, and dependency-failure behavior are unresolved.\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A timer measuring elapsed time from submission event to response receipt at the user boundary; a defined match oracle.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a user submits a search, the search system shall present the matching records ordered by [unresolved: creation time | last-modification time | other timestamp] from newest to oldest.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source states results should show \"the newest matching records.\" This is a sort-order obligation on the result presentation. \"Newest\" is ambiguous — the timestamp field and tie-break rule are unresolved (Q2). \"Show the newest\" could also mean return only the top-N newest rather than all matches sorted; that scope question is unresolved (Q3).\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The search system, at the user-facing response boundary.\n  - Responsible system: Search system.\n  - Actor: User (receiving search results).\n  - Affected interfaces: The user-facing result presentation interface.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The set of records matching the submitted search (as defined by Candidate 1). Each record must expose a timestamp field whose definition is unresolved (Q2).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the same user search submission as Candidate 1. The sort obligation applies to the result set produced in response to that submission.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — records are presented with the newest first. Alternate — a single matching record requires no ordering; an empty result set requires no ordering. Tie-break behavior for equal timestamps is unresolved (Q2).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: The timestamp field and its unit (epoch ms, ISO 8601, etc.) are unresolved. Whether all matches or only top-N are presented is unresolved (Q3).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Newest\" (Q2), scope of returned results (Q3), tie-break rule (Q2).\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A record store containing multiple matching records with distinct and equal timestamps; a user submits a search that matches those records.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The ordered sequence of records in the response, inspectable at the user-facing boundary.\n  - Expected result: Records appear in descending timestamp order (newest first); tie-break ordering is consistent with the agreed rule.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Unresolved — the timestamp field definition (Q2) and tie-break rule must be established. Pass requires that the first record in the response has a timestamp ≥ all others in the same response.\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Single matching record; all records sharing the same timestamp; empty result set.\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Access to the timestamp field value of each returned record at the response boundary, or an agreed oracle that can evaluate ordering correctness.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes, provided the timestamp field is included in the response.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**S1 — Failure-path companion (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* What the search system shall do when a dependency required to produce results is unavailable (e.g., the record store is unreachable).\n*Reason:* Without a defined failure response, two implementers will choose different visible behaviors (error message, partial results, silent empty set), making the system unverifiable at the boundary.\n*Suggested form (provisional):* If the search system cannot retrieve records within [unresolved: timeout], then the search system shall return an error response that does not present partial results as complete.\n\n**S2 — Boundary companion (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* Behavior when the submitted search is empty, malformed, or exceeds a defined length limit.\n*Reason:* The source does not constrain the input. An implementer must decide unilaterally whether to reject, interpret, or pass through these inputs.\n*Suggested form (provisional):* If the submitted search criteria are empty or malformed, then the search system shall return an informative response that identifies the input problem without exposing internal system detail.\n\n**S3 — Operational companion (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* A quantified response-time obligation.\n*Reason:* \"Quickly\" is the stated goal. Without a measurable threshold the requirement is unverifiable. This companion captures the performance obligation once Q1 is answered.\n*Suggested form (provisional, pending Q1):* When a user submits a search, the search system shall return the first results within [agreed threshold, e.g., 2 seconds] as measured from submission to first result visible at the user-facing boundary.\n\n**S4 — Optional consideration (Candidate 1).**\n*Missing behavior:* Whether results served from a cache may be stale relative to the current record store state, and what the maximum acceptable staleness window is.\n*Reason:* The source implies caching. If stale results are permissible, this is a visible behavioral contract (users may not see records created after the cache was populated). If not permissible, the cache TTL is an internal tuning detail with no behavioral relevance.\n*Ask:* Is serving results that do not reflect records created or modified within the last [N minutes] acceptable? (See Q6.)\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n**Finding 1 — Gap: quantified performance obligation is absent.**\nCandidate 1 references a response-time goal inherited from \"quickly\" in the source, but no measurable threshold appears in either candidate. S3 is suggested to close this gap once Q1 is answered.\n\n**Finding 2 — Overlap risk: Candidates 1 and 2 share the same trigger.**\nBoth candidates are triggered by \"user submits a search.\" They describe different responses (record retrieval vs. result ordering) and are intentionally split because they can be implemented and verified independently. No conflict; the split is deliberate. The combined behavior is: retrieve matching records AND present them ordered newest-first.\n\n**Finding 3 — Precedence question: cache staleness vs. result completeness.**\nIf a caching layer is used (the source's stated design intent), results returned by Candidate 1 may not include records added after the cache was populated. This creates a potential tension between the completeness of \"all matching records\" (Candidate 1) and any staleness window accepted in S4. Resolution requires an answer to Q6 and an explicit staleness bound or a requirement that results reflect the state of the record store at the time of submission.\n\n**Finding 4 — Gap: empty-result behavior is undefined.**\nNeither candidate specifies what the system returns when no records match the submitted search. S2 partially addresses malformed input; the empty-result case is covered by Q5 and needs an explicit requirement.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1.** \"Redis cache\" and \"GraphQL\" in the source are design proposals, not externally observable behavioral obligations. They are treated as rationale for the performance goal and are excluded from normative sentences. If either is intended as an external interface contract (e.g., the client is required to speak GraphQL), that changes the system boundary and an interface companion requirement would be needed.\n\n**A2.** \"Newest matching records\" is interpreted as a sort order over the full matching result set (newest first), not as a filter that returns only the top-N most recent records. This interpretation may be wrong; Q3 seeks confirmation.\n\n**A3.** \"After a user submits a search\" is interpreted as a discrete event-driven trigger, not an ongoing state. If the system is expected to continuously update results as new records arrive (live/streaming search), the pattern would change to state-driven or complex, and additional behavior would need to be defined.\n\n**A4.** The system boundary is the user-facing search interface. If the search system also exposes a machine-to-machine API boundary, additional interface companion requirements may be needed.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe source mixes two kinds of content:\n\n1. **Observable behavioral goals** — return matching records quickly, show the newest first. These are the basis for Candidates 1 and 2.\n\n2. **Implementation design ideas** — use Redis with a five-minute TTL, use GraphQL. These explain *why* the requester expects good performance but do not themselves describe user-visible behavior. A verifier cannot observe \"Redis\" or \"GraphQL\" at the user boundary. Including them in normative sentences would constrain the implementation without constraining the behavior, which is the opposite of good requirements practice.\n\nThe five-minute TTL, if it becomes a behavioral contract (e.g., users must not see records more than five minutes out of date), should be captured as a staleness bound in a supporting requirement (see S4 and Q6), not as an implementation detail.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking item |\n|-----------|--------|---------------|\n| Candidate 1 | needs clarification | Q1 (response-time threshold), Q4 (match definition), Q5 (empty-result response), Q6 (staleness) |\n| Candidate 2 | needs clarification | Q2 (timestamp field and tie-break), Q3 (full set vs. top-N) |\n\nNeither candidate is `ready for review`. Both verification contracts contain unresolved items that prevent a verifier from determining pass or fail. The primary blocking questions are Q1 (performance threshold) and Q2 (sort key definition). Once those are answered and the contracts are completed, the candidates should be re-evaluated against both the implementability and verification gates before advancing.\n\n---\n\nThe package has also been written to `artifacts/result.md`.\n\n**Key elicitation decisions made in this package:**\n\n| Source phrase | Treatment |\n|---|---|\n| \"Redis cache with a five-minute TTL\" | Design idea — kept out of normative sentences; surfaced in A1, S4, Finding 3, and Q6 |\n| \"GraphQL\" | Design idea — excluded from normative sentences; noted in A1 with a condition for when it would change the boundary |\n| \"return quickly\" | Flagged as unquantified; Q1 and S3 seek the measurable threshold |\n| \"newest matching records\" | Split into two independent obligations: result retrieval (C1) and sort order (C2); Q2 and Q3 resolve the ambiguity |","time":{"start":1791981972697,"end":1791982039703}}}
